# Swipewalk License

Version 1.0, applies to Swipewalk 0.4.2 and later.
Copyright (c) 2026 Swipewalk. All rights not granted below are reserved.

Swipewalk is free to use. This licence says what you may do with it, in plain words first and
legal terms where they matter. If a plain-word summary and a numbered section ever disagree, the
numbered section is what counts.

**In short:** you may use Swipewalk for any purpose this licence allows, including paid work for your own company or your
clients, on as many computers as you like, at no cost. You may pass on unmodified copies exactly as
we publish them, free of charge. You may not sell Swipewalk, offer it to others as a hosted or online
service, pass on changed copies, or take it apart to get at its source code.

## 1. What this covers

"Swipewalk" means the software we publish under this licence from version 0.4.2 on, in the form we
publish it: the Mac desktop app, the `swipewalk` command-line tool and its NuGet package, the
small helper apps and test harnesses Swipewalk installs on a
device, the sample app builds attached to releases, the Swipewalk extension for Visual Studio Code, and the documentation that comes with them. "We" and
"Swipewalk" mean the organisation Swipewalk, the copyright holder.

It does not cover:

- **Versions 0.1.0 to 0.4.1.** Those were released under the Apache License 2.0 and stay under it
  (section 8).
- **Third-party components** that Swipewalk includes. They keep their own licences (section 7).
- **Your apps, and what Swipewalk produces from them** (section 3).

## 2. What you may do

We give you a non-exclusive, worldwide, royalty-free licence to:

1. install and run Swipewalk on any number of computers you own or control, and on devices you are
   allowed to test;
2. use it for any purpose, including commercial work: testing your own apps, your employer's apps,
   or your clients' apps, including as a paid consultant or auditor;
3. read, print and share the documentation, and quote short parts of it with credit to Swipewalk;
4. give unmodified copies of the installers, packages and other downloads to other people, exactly as we publish
   them, with this licence and all notices kept with them, as long as you do not charge for the
   copy itself.

## 3. Your results are yours

Reports, `results.json` files, exports, screenshots and other files Swipewalk writes from your scans
belong to you or whoever owns the app that was scanned. You may keep them, change them, publish them
and give them to clients or buyers without asking us and without credit. We claim no rights in them.
Reports contain short passages of Swipewalk's own text (finding messages, fix guidance, the
laws-and-standards list); you may include those passages when you share a report.

## 4. What you may not do

You may not:

1. **sell, rent, lease or resell Swipewalk itself**, charge for access to it as a product, or
   **offer Swipewalk as a hosted, online or managed service** for others to use (for example a website,
   API or cloud service that runs Swipewalk scans for other people), whether you charge for it or not.
   Using Swipewalk yourself is allowed, including for paid audit, testing or consulting work on your
   clients' apps. Offering Swipewalk itself as a service that others use is not;
2. **distribute modified copies** of Swipewalk, or anything made by changing it or built from its
   parts, or put it into another product you distribute;
3. **reverse engineer, decompile or disassemble** Swipewalk, or try to get at its source code,
   except to the extent the law of your country expressly allows this despite this restriction (for
   example, to the limited extent European law allows for interoperability). This does not limit
   the rights third-party components give you (section 7). A few small parts, such as the iOS
   scanning harness and the helper scripts that run on your Mac, ship as source code because they are
   built or run on your computer. You may read, build and run them as part of using Swipewalk; the
   rest of this licence, including section 4.2, applies to them too;
4. **remove or hide** copyright, licence or disclaimer notices, or use the Swipewalk name or logo
   in a way that suggests we made, checked or endorse your product, report or conclusions;
5. use Swipewalk to scan apps you are not allowed to test. You are responsible for having the
   permission of the app's owner and for following the app's and the app store's terms.

## 5. Updates and changes to this licence

We may publish later versions of Swipewalk under a different licence. A copy you already have stays
under the licence it came with. We may update this licence text for new versions; the new text does
not take away rights you already have in a copy you downloaded.

## 6. No warranty and limit of liability

**Swipewalk is provided "as is", with no warranty of any kind**, express or implied, including that
it is accurate, complete, error-free, fit for a particular purpose or does not infringe anything.

**Results are not advice and not a verdict.** Automated checks find only some accessibility issues
and can be wrong: they can report things that are not problems and miss things that are. A report,
including one with no findings, does not show that an app meets WCAG, Section 508, EN 301 549, the
ADA or any other law, standard or contract. Laws and standards listed as "relevant to" a finding
are reference information only; ask a qualified person about legal questions. Manual testing by people using assistive technology is
still needed. See the disclaimer at <https://github.com/swipewalk/swipewalk/blob/main/DISCLAIMER.md>.

**To the extent the law allows**, Swipewalk and its authors are not liable for any loss or
damage arising from the use of, or inability to use, Swipewalk or its results, whether in contract,
tort or otherwise. This includes lost profits, lost data, damage to a device or an app, and
decisions, contract bids, certifications or legal positions based on a result. Nothing in this
licence excludes or limits liability that the law does not allow us to exclude or limit.

Swipewalk may change settings on a connected device while it works (for example the text size)
and puts them back afterwards. Use test devices where you can.

## 7. Third-party components

Swipewalk includes software from others. Each part keeps its own licence, which applies to that part
instead of this licence. Nothing here limits the rights those licences give you for those parts. The
full list, with licence names and links, is in `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md`, which is published with the documentation,
included in the NuGet package and carried in the sample app downloads. In summary:

- **axe-core** (Deque Systems), version 4.10.3, under the **Mozilla Public License 2.0**. It is
  included unmodified as `third-party/axe-core/axe.min.js`, with its own `LICENSE` file beside
  it. Its source code is available from its authors at
  <https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core/tree/v4.10.3>. If we ever change axe-core, the changed files
  stay under the MPL 2.0 and we will publish them.
- **Google's Accessibility Test Framework** and the AndroidX, Kotlin, Guava and related libraries
  compiled into the Android helper app, under the **Apache License 2.0**; JUnit under the
  **Eclipse Public License 1.0** (JUnit's source code is available from its authors at
  <https://github.com/junit-team/junit4>); Hamcrest and Protocol Buffers under **BSD 3-Clause**; `jsr305` under BSD 3-Clause per the JSR-305 project (its Maven metadata says Apache 2.0);
  jsoup and the Checker Framework qualifiers under the **MIT** licence.
- **The sample app's Android libraries** (AndroidX, Material, Kotlin, Gson and related libraries, 93 packages
  listed in `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md`), each a Microsoft .NET binding under the **MIT** licence around a library
  under its own licence: the **Apache License 2.0** for most; Glide under **BSD 2-Clause** (its disk cache under
  Apache 2.0, its GIF decoder under MIT); `jsr305` under BSD 3-Clause per the JSR-305 project (its package
  metadata says Apache 2.0).
- **Open Sans** and the **Noto Sans** fonts (Devanagari, Arabic, JP) under the **SIL Open Font
  License 1.1**. The fonts stay free to use and share under that licence; this licence does not
  restrict them.
- **.NET, .NET MAUI, Roslyn (Microsoft.CodeAnalysis) and PDFsharp** under the **MIT** licence.

Swipewalk also runs tools you install yourself (`adb`, Xcode and its command-line tools). Their own
terms apply to them.

## 8. Earlier versions: 0.1.0 to 0.4.1

Swipewalk 0.1.0 to 0.4.1 were released under the **Apache License 2.0**. We are not withdrawing that
grant. If you received one of those versions, source or binary, you keep every right the Apache
License 2.0 gave you for it, including the right to modify and redistribute that version under
that licence, now and in the future. The terms for those versions are the Apache License 2.0
(<https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0>). This licence applies only to 0.4.2 and later; later versions are
not covered by the Apache grant, even if they contain code that was also in an earlier version.
As copyright owner, Swipewalk may license the same code under different terms.

## 9. Ending of this licence

Your rights under this licence end automatically if you break it. When that happens you must stop
using Swipewalk and delete your copies. If you stop breaking it and we do not object within 30
days after you stop, your rights come back. Sections 3, 6, 7, 8 and 10 continue after the licence
ends. Ending this licence never affects rights under the Apache License 2.0 for versions 0.1.0 to
0.4.1.

